  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nA cold front begins moving eastward toward the region today, \nbut it doesn't look like it is going to make much progress as an\narea of short wave ridging aloft moves eastward as well. A few \nshowers or thunderstorms develop across western portions of the\nforecast area through early evening. These will then run out of\nsolar insolation, and into an area with warming mid level \ntemperatures.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nClosed low down to 995 mb will slam on the breaks as it fully \noccludes and cuts off from the upper-level flow. The result will be \na showery period later Tuesday onward. Will break down the \ndetails below, but in a nutshell, expect scattered-numerous \nshowers and embedded thunderstorms each day through at least \nFriday. Falling heights and surface-500 mb thickness, combined \nwith abundant cloud cover, suggests below average daytime \ntemperatures, however the humidity will be back as dew points \nsurge into the 60s.\n\nTuesday's precipitation chances will be in the west - primarily \nacross New Hampshire. Marginal daytime instability likely develops \nwith morning sunshine, but weak shear (0-6 km BWD < 20 kts), will \nhold back a formidable chance for organized/long-lived convection. \nLikely some topographically-enhanced pulse storms across the higher \nterrain. Should see these weaken around sundown given the limited \nshear.\n\nThe surface low marches closer on Wednesday. A TROWAL-enhanced band \nlikely lifts onshore around this time, but guidance remains \nundecided whether central New England is more directly impacted, or \nif it primarily stays further south. Thus, Wednesday has a range of \noutcomes from a washout to mostly dry, with the best odds for the \nlatter nearest to the Canadian border. What is more likely however \nis with the low center spinning to our southwest, increasingly \nonshore develops, which may usher in some rather cool conditions \nfor July, especially across Maine.\n\nFrom Thursday onward, impacts become less clear, especially outside \nof the hi-res guidance window, but the GFS and ECMWF are in rather \nstrong agreement through Friday the surface low slowly \nmeanders/dissipates in the vicinity of southern New England. With \nthe look of a cold pool aloft, likely we see diurnally/topographically\nforced downpours/thunderstorms Thu-Fri, focused each afternoon.\nMoist columns with marginal MUCAPE and weak shear suggest pulse\nstorms primarily posing a heavy rain risk rather than severe \nhazards. There also remains uncertainty how long the surface low\nmeanders, with the GFS suggesting improving conditions by \nSaturday, while the ECMWF is less optimistic. Current mean \nensemble QPF outputs are for 1-2\" regionwide, with a 90th \npercentile of 2-4\", with the gradient featuring the highest \ntotals across SW New Hampshire and the lowest in central Maine. \nKeep in mind however these are 3-4 day totals, so not overly \nconcerned about excessive rainfall at a glance, but still will \nmonitor closely.",
